From kimai at Princeton.Edu  Sat Jun 13 16:46:37 2015
From: kimai at Princeton.Edu (Kosuke Imai)
Date: Sat, 13 Jun 2015 10:46:37 -0400
Subject: [Mediation-information] Prop. Mediated vs. ACME (average).
In-Reply-To: <CAGmnqXj+sfKHtwgZjb4puHotS4QXnviyg1V+UP8kYuXc+g8g7A@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAGmnqXj+sfKHtwgZjb4puHotS4QXnviyg1V+UP8kYuXc+g8g7A@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CAFiYZ5yELPVswq1SGy9V9uHGH8y8kQF=QZ30Maj_9747sT+riQ@mail.gmail.com>

Prop Mediated is a ratio of two estimates, which are known to have a very
high variance especially when the average treatment effect is not precisely
estimated.  I would focus on the point estimate of this quantity rather
than its CI.  The most important thing is the ACME which have a much more
stable CI.

Kosuke Imai
Department of Politics
Princeton University
http://imai.princeton.edu

